{"step_index":6297,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-24T01:17:01Z","thinking":"Initial attempt involves a graceful shutdown through an HTTP endpoint, sending a shutdown request to the server. Following the request, the agent checks if the port is no longer listening, allowing the server time to exit cleanly.\n\n\nIf the initial shutdown attempt fails, the agent now terminates the tracked subprocess directly, regardless of the operating system, with robust error handling.  On Windows, the logic checks for orphaned processes listening on the target port and attempts to forcefully terminate them. Finally, if the port remains in a listening state after these attempts, a warning is logged.\n\n\nThe failure to stop suggests the port is still in use, possibly by an elevated process, requiring manual intervention to release it. Testing the server's health endpoint confirms whether the server is running and can respond to basic requests; if it is not running, the \"test\" will explicitly report that.\n\n\nNow, a chat completion test is performed, sending a basic \"PONG\" request to the server, measuring the round-trip latency. Successful responses are parsed for the reply content, indicating server availability, while failures include the HTTP status code and an error message to help identify problems, including errors. The health information collected earlier is also added to the response.\n\n\nThe server logging mechanism is examined. `ServerInstance` uses a logger callback, which is passed through `MultiServerManager` during initialization. The application leverages separate log storage for pipeline and dedicated server logs. The server logs are then sent to a front-end window.\n\n\n","tool_calls":[{"name":"view_file","args":{"AbsolutePath":"\"D:\\\\AI_Engineer\\\\Pharmacy-agy\\\\desktop_app\\\\backend.py\"","EndLine":"85","StartLine":"45","toolAction":"\"Checking backend.py logger setup\"","toolSummary":"\"View backend.py li